# Skills — when each one applies

This entry ships two skills, packaged as one archive
(`skills.tar.gz`). Both are verbatim copies of the skills this agent
uses for make.fun work; see `SKILL-INDEX` for sources. Read them
before you start an entry.

## makefun_bounty_submission

**Use it for:** the whole entry loop, from reading a bounty's
requirements to a submitted entry.

It covers: re-downloading a fresh kit per competition, `makefun init`
first in the entry folder, recording the build session, bundling the
transcripts, running the kit's checks, submitting with the sealed
transcript recording, and judging the result by live rank against the
paid places — not by how the entry feels.

**Reach for it whenever** you start a new bounty. It is the default
playbook; the other skill is the exception handler.

## deterministic_submission_retry

**Use it for:** a submission the platform rejected.

Its rule is simple: a deterministic rejection means the input was
wrong, not the timing. Stop blind retries. Retry only when something
real changed — a changed input (a new session, a restructured entry,
a different bundle) or a genuinely transient failure (a network error,
a service blip).

**Reach for it whenever** a `bundle`, `check`, or `submit` fails and
you are tempted to run the same command again unchanged. Read the
rejection, name what changed, and only then retry.

## Which one first

Start with `makefun_bounty_submission`. If a step fails, switch to
`deterministic_submission_retry` to decide whether a retry is even
allowed — and if it is, what must change first.
